WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.769 --> 00:00:45.250
In 1298, a manuscript was published that took
Western Europe by storm.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:49.809
Known today as the Travels of Marco Polo,
it detailed the Venetian’s adventures in

00:00:49.809 --> 00:00:53.710
Asia - from India, to Japan, to Sri Lanka.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:58.680
Impressive as those tales were, though, it
was the figure at the heart of Book Two who

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.060
really set Europe alight.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:07.340
The wise, ancient emperor who ruled a China
so wealthy, so enlightened, that it seemed

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:08.399
like Heaven on Earth.

00:01:08.399 --> 00:01:13.569
That great emperor’s name was Kublai Khan,
founder of the Yuan Dynasty and grandson of

00:01:13.569 --> 00:01:14.640
Genghis Khan.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.270
He would go down as one of the greatest rulers
in Asian history.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:24.060
At the time of Marco Polo’s visit, Kublai
Khan sat at the head of an empire so big,

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.189
it defies comprehension.

00:01:25.189 --> 00:01:29.840
A Mongol warrior trained from birth in the
art of war, he’d nonetheless grown into

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.590
an emperor who valued Buddhism, science, and
progress above conquest.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:39.560
A towering figure in both the Western and
Chinese imagination, this is the story of

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:47.960
Kublai Khan: the Mongol warrior who became
a Chinese emperor.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:49.159
The Great Khan

00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:54.659
When you hear the name Kublai Khan, we’re
guessing you also hear the incredibly famous

00:01:54.659 --> 00:01:56.939
opening of Samuel Taylor Coleridge’s poem:

00:01:56.939 --> 00:02:01.759
“In Xanadu did Kubla Khan, a stately pleasure
dome decree.”

00:02:01.759 --> 00:02:06.149
Aside from being two of the most-famous lines
in English, they also perfectly sum up the

00:02:06.149 --> 00:02:13.160
Western vision of the emperor: a semi-mythical
aesthete living a life of unparalleled elegance.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:17.980
Which means it can come as a shock to realize
Kublai Khan wasn’t born in a grand palace

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:21.700
and didn’t spend his life swanning around
in silk pajamas.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:27.720
No, when Kublai first opened his eyes on September
23, 1215, the sight that greeted him wasn’t

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.190
the rarefied air of the Chinese imperial court…

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:35.710
…but the mud and din and savagery of the
Mongol Empire.

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:39.290
Just nine years earlier, Genghis Khan had
united the rowdy, drunk nomadic herders of

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:45.010
the Mongolian steppe into a rowdy, drunk army
that was nonetheless one of the most-effective

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:46.690
fighting units on Earth.

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:50.360
Although the empire’s early days had been
marked by forward-thinking policies like banning

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.550
the kidnapping of women, that progressivism
had quickly given way to conquest.

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:59.540
In 1209, Genghis Khan invaded the neighboring
Xi Xia Kingdom, beginning the process that

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:04.760
would lead to his descendants controlling
the largest contiguous empire in history.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:11.010
By the time Kublai was born in 1215, Genghis
had flattened the Xi Xia, crushed the Jin

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:15.640
Dynasty, and captured Zhongdu - the city we’d
now call Beijing.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:20.920
We mention this because Kublai wasn’t just
some random kid born to some random Mongol.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:24.780
Kublai’s father was Tolui, the youngest
son of Genghis Khan.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.180
Well, the youngest legitimate son.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:32.090
Given that one in 200 men today are direct
descendants of Genghis Khan, we’re guessing

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:35.740
there were quite a few illegitimate younger
sons out there.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:41.760
Now this didn’t mean Kublai grew up bouncing
on Genghis Khan’s knee as he conquered the

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:42.760
world.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:45.520
In fact, Kublai barely saw his grandpa or
his father.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:50.530
Instead, he was mostly raised by his mother,
the Christian princess Sorkhotani Beki.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:53.620
This meant a somewhat unusual upbringing by
Mongol standards.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:58.160
For one thing, Sorkhotani was a ferociously
intelligent woman, and taught Kublai and his

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:04.080
brother Möngke to read at a time when literacy
came a distant second to being able to bring

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.170
down an antelope.

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:10.610
For another, she also made sure to expose
her boys to Chinese culture and philosophy.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:14.820
The impression this made on Kublai was so
great, it would eventually change the course

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:15.880
of Asian history.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:19.039
Still, Kublai wasn’t a pampered little prince.

00:04:19.039 --> 00:04:23.040
Before he was 10, he was a formidable horseman,
hunter, and fighter - all things the Mongols

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.449
needed if they were to keep on conquering.

00:04:25.449 --> 00:04:29.320
And conquering was by now what the Mongols
were all about.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:34.259
When Kublai was just five, the Central Asian
Khwarezm Empire made the fatal of insulting

00:04:34.259 --> 00:04:35.259
Genghis Khan.

00:04:35.259 --> 00:04:39.479
By the time Kublai was ten, Khwarezmia had
fallen, leaving the Mongols in charge of an

00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:44.190
empire that stretched from the Sea of Japan
to the Caspian Sea - a distance similar to

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:47.870
that separating Memphis, Tennessee from Honolulu.

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:52.560
Little did young Kublai know it, but that
empire was about to fall into his family’s

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:53.560
hands.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:58.909
On August 18, 1227, Genghis Khan died of injuries
sustained falling off a horse.

00:04:58.909 --> 00:05:03.620
In the aftermath of the great Khan’s death,
control of the empire briefly passed to Kublai’s

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:04.999
father, Tolui.

00:05:04.999 --> 00:05:07.710
It was the family’s first brush with immense
power.

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:09.360
It certainly wouldn’t be their last.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:16.599
The Age of Expansion

00:05:16.599 --> 00:05:22.349
In the wake of Genghis Khan’s death, a kuriltai,
or great gathering of Mongol leaders was called.

00:05:22.349 --> 00:05:27.460
There, the decision was made that Tolui should
hand control of the empire over to Genghis’s

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.199
other son, Ögedei.

00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:31.410
This was something of a weird choice.

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:36.309
Ögedei was a guy so dedicating to drunkenness
that, when other Mongol leaders ordered him

00:05:36.309 --> 00:05:41.270
to cut down to just three cups of wine a day,
he started drinking from cups roughly the

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:42.520
size of bathtubs.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:45.370
He also had no practical experience of conquering.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:48.650
What Ögedei did have, though, was an ability
to delegate.

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:50.349
His view of the empire was:

00:05:50.349 --> 00:05:55.349
“You can conquer an empire on horseback,
but you cannot govern it on horseback.”

00:05:55.349 --> 00:06:00.500
So, while great generals like Subutai were
sent West to keep conquering, anyone with

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:04.750
a more civilized bent of mind stayed home
to keep things in order.

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:08.770
It was this philosophy that led to Kublai
getting his first practical experience of

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:09.770
ruling.

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:15.240
In 1236, Ögedei granted Kublai a fiefdom
of 10,000 homes in modern Hebei.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:19.800
At first, the 21-year old Kublai was all like
“booooooooooorrrrrrrrring.”

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:25.309
He ignored his duties, sending agents to do
all the burdensome stuff like collecting taxes.

00:06:25.309 --> 00:06:29.819
Unfortunately, these agents were exactly as
competent as you’d expect a bunch of Mongol

00:06:29.819 --> 00:06:35.059
raiders to be, and the twin threats of taxation
and terror soon left Kublai’s province almost

00:06:35.059 --> 00:06:36.059
empty.

00:06:36.059 --> 00:06:40.259
It was only at the end of the decade that
Ögedei seems to have told Kublai, “dude,

00:06:40.259 --> 00:06:46.770
use that province or lose it,” and Kublai
finally started acting like a ruler.

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:53.650
But while the role may have been forced on
him, there’s no doubt he excelled at it.

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:58.400
With Kublai now on the scene, all those incompetent
tax collectors were fired, and replaced by

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.169
experienced Chinese officials.

00:07:00.169 --> 00:07:04.370
At the same time, Kublai began consulting
Chinese advisors more and more.

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:07.819
Although they were just a part of his local
court - a court that also included Christians

00:07:07.819 --> 00:07:11.360
and Muslims - they soon edged the other voices
out.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:16.039
It seems that Kublai just couldn’t get over
his childhood fascination with all things

00:07:16.039 --> 00:07:17.039
Chinese.

00:07:17.039 --> 00:07:19.129
For the other Mongols, this was shocking.

00:07:19.129 --> 00:07:24.939
Who would voluntarily exchange manly Mongol
traditions like hunting and fighting for wussy

00:07:24.939 --> 00:07:27.669
Chinese ones like tea and reading?

00:07:27.669 --> 00:07:30.139
But Kublai wasn’t to be deterred.

00:07:30.139 --> 00:07:35.129
He became interested in Buddhism, began to
appreciate Confucianism - including the revolutionary

00:07:35.129 --> 00:07:40.379
idea that the well-being of an empire’s
subjects was just as important as that of

00:07:40.379 --> 00:07:41.379
its rulers.

00:07:41.379 --> 00:07:44.930
Still, these interests seemed laughably irrelevant
to other Mongols.

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:49.800
The empire was currently pushing into Europe,
the unconquered south of China forgotten about.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:54.870
What need would the Mongols have for pretentious
Chinese guff now?

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:57.089
Yeah, about that.

00:07:57.089 --> 00:08:00.029
On December 11, 1241, Ögedei died of a stroke.

00:08:00.029 --> 00:08:04.349
At that precise moment, the Mongols were laying
waste to Eastern Europe.

00:08:04.349 --> 00:08:09.839
Kyiv had been sacked, Krakow put to the sword,
and raiding parties were knocking at the gates

00:08:09.839 --> 00:08:10.899
of Vienna.

00:08:10.899 --> 00:08:16.529
Then word arrived of Ögedei’s death, and
the conquerors simply turned around and went

00:08:16.529 --> 00:08:17.529
home.

00:08:17.529 --> 00:08:18.779
At least, that’s the story.

00:08:18.779 --> 00:08:22.999
Some historians think the retreat was sounded
for other reasons, and Ögedei’s death was

00:08:22.999 --> 00:08:24.819
just a convenient excuse.

00:08:24.819 --> 00:08:29.159
Whatever the truth, the upshot was that Europe
was forgotten about while the messy issue

00:08:29.159 --> 00:08:32.099
of Ögedei’s successor was sorted.

00:08:32.099 --> 00:08:37.020
After a few years of quarreling, it was eventually
decided that Kublai’s older brother, Möngke,

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:38.229
should be great Khan.

00:08:38.229 --> 00:08:42.700
And Möngke was a guy as obsessed with China
as Kublai.

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:47.430
From now on, the Mongol focus wasn’t gonna
be on rampaging further and further into Europe…

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:53.270
But on conquering the last independent parts
of China.

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:56.839
In Xanadu…

00:08:56.839 --> 00:09:01.250
No sooner had Möngke been declared great
Khan than he gave Kublai two very important

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:02.250
things.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:03.830
One was Northern China.

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:08.650
Yep, the Mongol Empire in 1251 was so vast
that Möngke could basically gift half of

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:12.510
China to his brother and say “hope you like!”

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:16.430
The second was an order, one designed to test
Kublai’s mettle.

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:20.000
Möngke wanted Kublai to conquer the Dali
Kingdom.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:24.320
At this point, Kublai had never run a military
campaign, and there was no guarantee he wouldn’t

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:27.560
turn out to be the Mongol equivalent of Mister
Bean.

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:31.300
It took Kublai a whole year to prepare to
fight the Dali.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:36.280
A whole year in which he practiced tactics,
and read great Chinese thinkers on the arts

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:37.280
of war.

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:42.200
Finally, in 1253, Kublai saddled up, got his
army together, and rode into the Dali Kingdom.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:43.770
The conquest took three years.

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:47.940
But it wasn’t three years marked by the
usual Mongol atrocities.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:51.800
Kublai by now firmly believed what his Chinese
advisors had taught him about a ruler’s

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:53.560
codependence with the ruled.

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:57.580
Which meant not killing new subjects during
conquest, but treating them with mercy.

00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:02.630
For the Mongols, this was seriously cuckoo
stuff; like if a modern general suggested

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:07.060
his army tried replacing its bullets with
Black Forest gateau.

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:09.200
But Kublai was serious.

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:13.580
Terror was to be toned down, and pillaging
was out.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:16.660
And so a new era of Mongol warfare was born.

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:19.290
By 1256, the Dali Kingdom had fallen.

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:23.740
Buoyed by his brother’s success, Möngke
began planning an invasion of southern China,

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:26.030
where the Song Dynasty still held out.

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:30.750
But such an undertaking required time, time
in which Kublai wasn’t just gonna park his

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:34.290
thumb up his ass and wait for his brother
to say “go”.

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:39.620
So Kublai left Dali and returned north to
embark on his most-famous project of all.

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:42.690
Xanadu is legendary in the English-speaking
world.

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:47.390
Although this is mostly due to Coleridge’s
poem, it deserves the hype.

00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:52.290
Kublai’s new capital was designed on Chinese
principles of feng shui, perfectly placed

00:10:52.290 --> 00:10:58.440
in relation to mountains and rivers, between
the agricultural lowlands and Mongolian steppe.

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:03.220
Great walls and towers enclosed a city of
200,000 people, all laid out on a classic

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:04.470
Chinese square plan.

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:10.130
At its heart sat a palace almost beyond imagination,
a “stately pleasure dome” complete with

00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:11.850
its own hunting grounds.

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:17.170
It was an expression of Kublai’s love for
Chinese style… but also of his power.

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:19.530
Power that was growing by the minute.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:26.700
In 1258, Kublai invited leaders of both Buddhist
thought and Daoism to Xanadu for a grand religious

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:27.700
debate.

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:32.260
There, he listened to both sides, before eventually
declaring that Buddhism had won and making

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:34.480
it Northern China’s official religion.

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:38.890
It a decision that would play a huge part
in Buddhism’s spread across East Asia.

00:11:38.890 --> 00:11:42.810
But as Kublai’s stature grew, Möngke began
to take notice.

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:48.430
The same year as the religious debate, he
sent agents to Xanadu, who began purging Kublai’s

00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:51.100
Chinese advisors on trumped-up charges.

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:55.050
For a moment, it looked like there could be
civil war between the brothers.

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:59.200
But Möngke backed off at the last moment,
realizing he needed his brother for the coming

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:00.730
fight against the Song.

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:04.640
They patched things up and, in 1259, finally
invaded southern China.

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:07.440
Sadly, though, the civil war hadn’t been
stopped.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:11.740
It would now just take place between different
siblings.

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:14.480
On August 11, Möngke died while besieging
a Song city.

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:17.770
Immediately, two candidates for his successor
presented themselves.

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:23.010
Up in Mongolia proper, Kublai’s younger
brother Arigböge was at the head of a conservative

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:26.300
faction, displeased with Möngke’s China
war.

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:30.840
Down in the south, there was Kublai, even
now fighting the war his brother had so long

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:31.840
dreamed of.

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:35.260
There was only one way a Khan could ever be
chosen between the two siblings.

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:37.180
They were going to have to fight.

00:12:37.180 --> 00:12:42.740
The Wrath of the Khan

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:47.680
If anyone ever had any doubt about Kublai’s
ambitions, they were quickly dispelled after

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:48.680
Möngke’s death.

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:51.910
As soon as he could, Kublai made a peace treaty
with the Song.

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:58.100
Then he quick marched back up north to Xanadu
and, on May 5, 1260, called a kuriltai.

00:12:58.100 --> 00:13:03.870
It ended with Kublai being elected great Khan
and a proclamation drawn up in classical Chinese.

00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:08.410
But while Kublai’s meeting had named him
leader, this wasn’t the same as him actually

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:09.730
leading the Mongols.

00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:14.040
Up in Karakorum, Arigböge responded to the
news by essentially going “oh, yeah?”

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:18.380
and holding his own kuriltai which named him
great Khan.

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:19.430
It was a stalemate.

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:23.600
Kublai now controlled the biggest, richest
part of the empire, while Arigböge ruled

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:24.600
the homeland.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:28.170
It’s arguable that this stalemate was never
really resolved.

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:32.680
Although Kublai defeated Arigböge’s forces
in 1264, the north of the empire remained

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:34.150
out of his control.

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:39.130
The conservative faction replaced Arigböge
with a guy called Kaidu, and Kaidu basically

00:13:39.130 --> 00:13:42.470
just did his own thing, like he was separate
from Kublai’s empire.

00:13:42.470 --> 00:13:47.050
It’s around this point that we can stop
thinking of the Mongol Empire as a single

00:13:47.050 --> 00:13:51.770
entity, and more like the collection of Khanates
it would eventually become.

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:55.930
Still, nominal control of the homeland was
good enough for Kublai.

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:58.920
Arigböge defeated, he went straight back
to war with the Song Dynasty.

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:01.320
It was a hell of a fight.

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:05.720
Although only in control of southern China,
the Song could still call up an army of one

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:06.720
million men.

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:08.850
They had gunpowder, catapults, siege engines.

00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:11.090
A navy with the biggest ships in human history.

00:14:11.090 --> 00:14:14.800
It was a fight Kublai had no guarantee he
could win.

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:16.780
So why did he bother?

00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:18.260
The reason was simple.

00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:21.450
Kublai didn’t just want to make the Song
submit.

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:23.520
He wanted to reunify China.

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:28.140
Prior to Kublai Khan, the last time China
had been unified had been under the Tang Dynasty,

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:30.700
who’d fallen 360 years before.

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:34.330
To give you some idea of how ridiculously
long ago that was, traveling back in time

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:37.740
360 years from today would land you in 1660.

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:41.690
In 1660, Oliver Cromwell had only just died.

00:14:41.690 --> 00:14:43.760
New York was still New Amsterdam.

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:46.730
Louis XIV was on the French throne.

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:52.060
Kublai’s dream of reuniting China was like
you meeting someone today who dreams of returning

00:14:52.060 --> 00:14:54.210
the Thirteen Colonies to England.

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:57.430
It was mad, laughable.

00:14:57.430 --> 00:14:59.820
It was also something he totally succeeded
at.

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:04.720
By 1271, Kublai had established a new capital
at modern-day Beijing, and renamed his empire

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:08.230
in the Chinese style, declaring it the Yuan
Dynasty.

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:13.330
By the mid-1270s, Song generals were defecting,
swearing allegiance to the new Yuan emperor.

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:19.560
At last, on March 28, 1276, the boy emperor
of the Song was captured and taken as prisoner

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:20.560
to Beijing.

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:24.970
Although Song loyalists would fight on for
three more years, the writing was on the wall.

00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:28.670
The final end came on March 19, 1279.

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:32.710
On that day, a titanic naval battle took place
near modern Macu.

00:15:32.710 --> 00:15:36.580
When it was finally over, there was no-one
left to oppose Kublai Khan.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:40.910
Aged 63, the great Khan had at last finished
his brother’s work.

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:44.860
China was now conquered, reunified, and at
the mercy of the Mongols.

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:48.420
Only, the other Mongols didn’t quite see
it that way.

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:53.320
In the course of the conquest, Kublai’s
Yuan Dynasty had begun to take on more and

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:57.740
more Chinese characteristics, until it seemed
completely alien.

00:15:57.740 --> 00:16:01.790
No-one knew what was coming next, but they
may have agreed on one thing.

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:07.210
Whatever form Yuan rule took, it certainly
wasn’t going to be Mongol in nature.

00:16:07.210 --> 00:16:11.930
A Traveller from an Antique Land

00:16:11.930 --> 00:16:17.840
In 1275, three bedraggled men arrived at Kublai’s
Khan’s vast marble palace.

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:21.930
They came from the distant city of Venice,
over 7,500 km away.

00:16:21.930 --> 00:16:27.060
The older two were merchants, while the younger
was a lad of barely twenty with a gift for

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:28.060
languages.

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:29.490
They’d come intending only to stay a short
while.

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:35.440
But when the Khan saw them, he was so impressed
with the youngest that he made them stay for

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:37.280
16 whole years.

00:16:37.280 --> 00:16:39.510
The name of that young man?

00:16:39.510 --> 00:16:42.460
As you’ve already guessed, it was Marco
Polo.

00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:46.870
And it’s his account of Kublai’s court
that still forms the basis of how we see the

00:16:46.870 --> 00:16:48.020
Yuan Dynasty.

00:16:48.020 --> 00:16:51.790
By the time Polo presented himself to the
great Khan, the growing empire was already

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:53.130
in full swing.

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:57.650
Under the Yuan, China entered a period of
incredible prosperity, but one that wasn’t

00:16:57.650 --> 00:17:02.050
quite the uncomplicated picture of perfection
that Polo painted.

00:17:02.050 --> 00:17:06.000
On the good side, Kublai was a ruler who was
seriously into innovation.

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:10.390
Under his watch, everything from an empire-wide
postal service to the issuing of paper money

00:17:10.390 --> 00:17:11.390
was established.

00:17:11.390 --> 00:17:14.720
But Yuan China wasn’t just about physical
improvements.

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:17.380
Kublai was also a leader in innovative thinking.

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:19.130
Freedom of religion was embraced.

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:23.319
Any language from around the empire was permitted
at court, and Kublai even hired a team to

00:17:23.319 --> 00:17:27.301
create an artificial language which would
bind his subjects together - although this

00:17:27.301 --> 00:17:28.301
never caught on.

00:17:28.301 --> 00:17:29.900
There were scientific advances, too.

00:17:29.900 --> 00:17:34.620
Kublai had accurate maps made of China, established
institutes of medicine, and improved the calendar.

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:37.550
He also turned Chinese society on its head.

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:42.690
Under the Song, artisans and doctors had been
discriminated against as useless idiots who

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:48.170
spent time doing stuff with their hands rather
than refining their minds with Confucian poetry.

00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:49.720
But Kublai flipped that.

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:53.920
He handed artisans enormous tax breaks, promoted
doctors and astronomers.

00:17:53.920 --> 00:17:58.920
The result was an economic boom that dragged
reunified China into the new era.

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:02.890
But Kublai’s outward successes masked some
deeper failures.

00:18:02.890 --> 00:18:05.970
One was a state policy of racial discrimination.

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:10.000
While Kublai played the part of the Chinese
emperor, wearing traditional robes and allowing

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:15.380
himself to be carried around by servants,
the reality was that his China was deeply

00:18:15.380 --> 00:18:17.570
discriminatory towards Chinese.

00:18:17.570 --> 00:18:19.700
The Yuan had four social classes.

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:23.330
At the top were Mongols, who held all the
key posts.

00:18:23.330 --> 00:18:27.790
Second were useful foreigners, like Marco
Polo or the Khan’s Muslim treasurers.

00:18:27.790 --> 00:18:31.490
Third were northern Chinese, who shouldered
a heavy tax burden.

00:18:31.490 --> 00:18:35.680
And fourth were the southern Chinese, who
were considered lower than vermin.

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:41.340
The result was millions of Chinse crushed
by taxes, and treated like strangers in their

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:42.340
own land.

00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:47.540
On a personal level, too, Kublai wasn’t
quite the serene emperor of popular imagination.

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:51.750
The Khan drank like a fish, ate like a pig,
and screwed like a rabbit.

00:18:51.750 --> 00:18:56.670
The result was that he eventually transformed
into a fat, gout-ridden, horny blob prone

00:18:56.670 --> 00:18:58.650
to outbursts of temper.

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:02.970
And yet, for all these faults, there’s no
doubting Kublai’s legacy.

00:19:02.970 --> 00:19:08.880
In Chinese official historiography, the Yuan
era is regarded not as an Interregnum, but

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:14.940
as part of the imperial story - an honor only
afforded to one other foreign dynasty: the

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:15.940
Qing.

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:21.010
But while Kublai’s rule at home might be
complicated, there’s no such shades of gray

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:22.390
regarding his foreign policy.

00:19:22.390 --> 00:19:27.840
That’s because his time as emperor was taken
up not with being a great administrator, a

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:30.590
powerful warrior, or even a champion drunk…

00:19:30.590 --> 00:19:34.840
…but with an endless series of disastrous
wars.

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:38.330
The Other Great Khan

00:19:38.330 --> 00:19:44.480
One of the most-interesting things about Kublai
is that there always seem to have been two

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:47.020
people in his head, fighting for control.

00:19:47.020 --> 00:19:51.470
One was Kublai the Yuan Emperor, who wanted
nothing more than to follow his childhood

00:19:51.470 --> 00:19:55.250
obsession with all things Chinese, and settle
down as a wise ruler.

00:19:55.250 --> 00:20:00.460
The other was Kublai Khan, the Mongol warrior,
who felt his life should be taken up with

00:20:00.460 --> 00:20:01.460
conquest.

00:20:01.460 --> 00:20:06.820
Sadly for Kublai’s prosperity, in his later
years it was Kublai the warrior who won out.

00:20:06.820 --> 00:20:11.309
We say “sadly” because, despite his early-life
victories against the Dali, the Song, and

00:20:11.309 --> 00:20:16.880
Arigböge, late-life Kublai was a real putz
when it came to conquering.

00:20:16.880 --> 00:20:21.860
There were his two invasions of Japan, in
1274 and 1281, which both times resulted in

00:20:21.860 --> 00:20:27.570
typhoons wrecking his entire navy - an incident
which led to the Japanese concept of the “Divine

00:20:27.570 --> 00:20:29.480
Wind” or kamikaze.

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:33.460
There were his two invasions of Vietnam, and
his two invasions of Burma, both of which

00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:38.140
resulted in Pyrrhic victories thanks to their
sheer, insane cost.

00:20:38.140 --> 00:20:42.820
Finally, there was Kublai’s 1292 invasion
of Java, which ended with the victorious Mongols

00:20:42.820 --> 00:20:47.510
so riddled with disease that they were forced
to retreat after a single year.

00:20:47.510 --> 00:20:52.679
It was a disappointing end to a dizzying career,
like riding a rollercoaster that reaches its

00:20:52.679 --> 00:20:57.580
highest peak only to suddenly shut down and
leave you stuck and disappointed.

00:20:57.580 --> 00:21:02.770
But it’s likely that, by the time of the
Java disaster, Kublai no longer cared.

00:21:02.770 --> 00:21:08.270
In 1281 and 1285, the Yuan emperor had lost
first his favorite wife and then his eldest

00:21:08.270 --> 00:21:09.270
son.

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:13.280
This one-two punch of tragedy seems to have
floored Kublai, who withdrew from the day-to-day

00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:17.600
running of the empire, instead drowning his
sorrows in drinking and feasting.

00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:21.750
By the time he finally allowed Marco Polo
to leave his court, the one-time warrior was

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:28.820
nothing but a fat, unhappy man, trapped in
a vast palace as luxurious as it was empty.

00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:32.740
Kublai Khan finally died on February 18, 1294.

00:21:32.740 --> 00:21:37.000
His body was buried in a secret place somewhere
in Mongolia.

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:41.400
As with his grandfather, Genghis Khan, his
remains have never been found.

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:45.600
In the years after Kublai’s death, two important
things happened that would ensure his place

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:46.600
in history.

00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:51.679
The first was that Marco Polo’s book appeared,
forever fixing an image of the Khan as a wise

00:21:51.679 --> 00:21:53.160
and benevolent ruler.

00:21:53.160 --> 00:21:56.179
The second was the collapse of the Yuan Dynasty.

00:21:56.179 --> 00:22:00.970
Although Kublai’s descendants ruled China
peacefully for 30 years, in 1324 a series

00:22:00.970 --> 00:22:04.940
of uprisings marked the beginning of the Dynasty’s
decline.

00:22:04.940 --> 00:22:10.010
Come 1368, the Yuan were so weak that the
Ming were able to conquer China and establish

00:22:10.010 --> 00:22:11.480
their own dynasty.

00:22:11.480 --> 00:22:16.160
In the aftermath, the Mongols retreated back
to the steppe, never again playing an important

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:17.190
part in history.

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:20.090
But the same can’t be said of Kublai Khan.

00:22:20.090 --> 00:22:24.740
In 1644, the Ming in turn fell to Manchu invaders:
the Qing.

00:22:24.740 --> 00:22:29.690
As the new masters of China, the Qing looked
back to the last foreign dynasty as their

00:22:29.690 --> 00:22:33.260
inspiration; as the golden age they hoped
to emulate.

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:38.830
In the story of Kublai Khan, they found the
template for their own Dynasty - a Dynasty

00:22:38.830 --> 00:22:40.850
that would last until 1911.

00:22:40.850 --> 00:22:46.179
Today, Kublai’s name still survives - less
as that of an historic figure, and more as

00:22:46.179 --> 00:22:50.340
a byword for something romantic, poetic and
mystical.

00:22:50.340 --> 00:22:55.309
Yet even this is more than than that afforded
to any other Mongol leader apart from Genghis

00:22:55.309 --> 00:22:56.309
Khan.

00:22:56.309 --> 00:22:58.950
More even than perhaps any other Chinese emperor.

00:22:58.950 --> 00:23:03.330
Kublai Khan the man may be long dead, but
the stories of his exploits, the influence

00:23:03.330 --> 00:23:25.570
his life had on others, ensure that his name
will live on forever.

